    "evidence_text": "Here, we examine the effects of smokeless tobacco extracts on human oral fibroblasts.\n\nThus, our findings provide new knowledge of the underlying mechanism of cancer metastasis.\n\nmiR-196 may serve as a promising marker for better oral cancer management.\n\nHuman epithelium contains keratin, which is expressed during differentiation.\n\nThese observations were further investigated by immunohistochemical examination.\n\nMost K19-positive cancers appeared to develop with little associating SIN.\n\nAlphavbeta6-Fyn signaling promotes oral cancer progression.\n\nHowever, the mechanism by which the integrin beta6 promotes oral tumor progression is not well understood.\n\nThe high number of CD68-positive macrophages was correlated with poor overall survival.\n\nAberrant expression of K4 and K13 was associated with morphological changes in the affected oral epithelium.\n\nThese keratins, especially K4, may be useful for pathological diagnosis.\n\nThus, p62 is an anti-inflammatory tumor suppressor that acts through the modulation of metabolism in the tumor stroma.\n\nGene expression analysis by cDNA microarray in oral squamous cell carcinoma.\n\nComprehensive gene expression profiling is essential for understanding OSCC.   \n\n\nRESULTS Forty-seven genes with altered expression among all cases were extracted.",
